.TH SNOOZE 1 "2026-08-15" "snooze 2.2" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
snooze \- temporarily mute Zabbix hosts and hostgroups
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B snooze
.RI [ DURATION ] " " [ TARGETS ...]
.br
.B snooze mute
.RI [ DURATION ] " " [ TARGETS ...]
.br
.B snooze plan
.I TIME
.RI [ DURATION ] " " [ TARGETS ...]
.br
.B snooze extend
.I DURATION
.RI [ TARGETS ...]
.br
.B snooze unmute
.RI [ TARGETS ...]
.br
.B snooze list
.BR hosts | groups | active | plans
.br
.B snooze list suppressed
.RI [ TARGETS ...]
.br
.B snooze status
.br
.B snooze cleanup
.br
.B snooze help
.br
.B snooze version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B snooze
mutes Zabbix hosts and hostgroups by creating time\-boxed Zabbix
.I Maintenance
windows via the Zabbix JSON\-RPC API (Bearer token auth, requires
.B Zabbix >= 6.4
\- older versions expect
.I auth
in the request body instead of a Bearer header), and can remove them again
just as easily.
.PP
Each target gets its own maintenance window with a unique name
(\fIsnooze:<host>\fR for hosts, \fIsnooze:@<group>\fR for hostgroups). Muting
an already\-muted target updates the existing window in place
.RB ( maintenance.update )
instead of deleting and recreating it, so the target is never briefly
unmuted mid\-operation; running
.B mute
again therefore extends/updates the mute idempotently.
.PP
If the first argument is a known command
.RB ( mute ", " plan ", " extend ", " unmute ", " list ", " status ", "
.BR cleanup / prune ", " help ),
that command runs. Otherwise the entire input is treated as arguments to
.BR mute ,
so
.B "snooze 2h host1"
and
.B "snooze mute 2h host1"
are equivalent. With no arguments at all, the local host's FQDN is muted
for the configured default duration.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "mute [" DURATION "] [" TARGETS ...]
Mute the given targets (or the local host, if none given) for
.IR DURATION ,
or the configured default duration if none is given. Implied when the
first argument isn't a known command.
.TP
.BI "plan " TIME " [" DURATION "] [" TARGETS ...]
Like
.BR mute ,
but the maintenance window starts at
.I TIME
instead of immediately \- see
.B SCHEDULED START
below. Not shown by
.B "list active"
until it has actually started; see
.BR "list plans" .
.TP
.BI "extend " DURATION " [" TARGETS ...]
Extend an existing mute by
.I DURATION
from its current end (or from now, if it already expired).
.I DURATION
is mandatory here \- there is no default\-duration fallback for
.BR extend .
.TP
.BI "unmute [" TARGETS ...]
Remove a mute by deleting its maintenance window.
.TP
.B "list hosts"
List all hosts known to Zabbix.
.TP
.B "list groups"
List all hostgroups.
.TP
.B "list active"
Show already\-started snooze maintenance windows, with remaining time, end
time, and reason. Alias:
.BR status .
.TP
.B "list plans"
Show windows scheduled via
.B plan
that haven't started yet.
.TP
.BI "list suppressed [" TARGETS ...]
Show Zabbix problems currently suppressed by snooze's maintenance windows
(matched via the specific
.IR maintenanceid ,
not any unrelated Zabbix maintenance). With no targets, covers every window
snooze currently manages. Answers: "what would alert immediately if I
.B unmute
right now?"
.TP
.B cleanup
Delete all snooze\-managed windows whose end lies in the past (Zabbix
doesn't do this on its own). Alias:
.BR prune .
.TP
.B help
Show usage (also
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help ).
.TP
.B version
Show version and author (also
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version ).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-F, \-\-file " FILE
Read targets from
.I FILE
(one token per line,
.B #
starts a comment;
.B \-
reads from stdin).
.TP
.B "\-Y, \-\-yes"
Skip the confirmation prompt that would otherwise appear above
.I confirm_threshold
targets.
.TP
.BI "\-\-reason " TEXT
Set the maintenance window's reason/comment, overriding the configured
default.
.I {user}
and
.I {host}
placeholders are substituted at runtime.
.PP
Everything after a bare
.B \-\-
is treated as a target, even if it looks like a flag.
.PP
Piped or redirected input is read automatically, even without \fB\-F \-\fR:
whenever stdin is not a TTY,
.B snooze
reads it the same way
.B "\-F \-"
would and adds whatever targets it finds to whatever was given on the
command line. An interactive terminal (no pipe) is left alone, so a bare
.B snooze
never blocks waiting for input that was never sent, e.g.:
.IP
.nf
echo host1 host2 host3 | snooze
.fi
.SH SCHEDULED START
.B "snooze plan"
accepts
.I TIME
in one of two formats:
.TP
.B HH:MM
Today's date. If that time of day has already passed today, this is an
error (no silent roll\-over to tomorrow) \- give the date explicitly instead.
.TP
.B DD.MM.YYYY\ HH:MM
A fixed day and time. Leading zeros on day/month are optional. Given
unquoted, date and time arrive as two separate shell arguments and are
still accepted; quoted as one argument works the same way.
.PP
A
.I TIME
in the past is an error in either form (exit status 2).
.SH DURATION
Format:
.I N
immediately followed by
.IR SUFFIX ,
no separator (e.g.
.IR 30m ", " 4h ", " 2d ).
.TP
.B m, min
Minutes.
.TP
.B h
Hours.
.TP
.B d
Days.
.TP
.B w
Weeks (7 days).
.TP
.B M
Months (30 days).
.PP
Note the case: lowercase
.B m
/
.B min
are minutes, uppercase
.B M
is a month. Examples:
.BR 30m ", " 30min ", " 4h ", " 2d ", " 1w ", " 1M .
.SH TARGETS
Hosts, glob patterns, regexes, and
.B @
\-prefixed hostgroups may be freely mixed in one call.
.TP
.B Hosts
Names without a dot get the configured domain appended (e.g.
.I prd\-mail\-5
becomes
.IR prd\-mail\-5.domain.tld ).
.TP
.B Glob
May contain
.BR * ", " ? ", and " [...] ,
resolved against the live Zabbix host list, e.g.
.IR prd\-mail\-* .
.TP
.B Regex
Prefixed with
.BR ~ ,
e.g.
.IR ~^prd\-mail\-\ed+$ .
Matched against the technical hostname.
.TP
.B Hostgroups
Prefixed with
.BR @ .
Quote them if the name contains spaces or slashes:
.IR "\(dq@Servers/Linux\(dq" .
Glob/regex apply only to hosts, not groups.
.PP
If a glob/regex matches many hosts, or many targets are given directly,
.B snooze
asks for confirmation above
.I confirm_threshold
targets;
.B \-Y
skips this. Without a controlling terminal (e.g. in a script or cron job),
exceeding the threshold without
.B \-Y
is a hard error instead of hanging.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Settings come from an INI file (a single
.B [snooze]
section). The first readable file from this list wins:
.IP
.nf
\&./snooze.conf
~/.snooze.conf
/etc/snooze.conf
.fi
.PP
Keys:
.IR url ", " token ", " domain ", " duration ", " prefix ", " timeout ,
.IR reason ", " confirm_threshold ", " retries ", and " lang .
See
.I /etc/snooze.conf.example
for the full annotated reference. Every key can also be set via an
environment variable (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
.SH LANGUAGE
All output, including this text's counterpart from
.BR "snooze help" ,
is available in German and English. Chosen at startup from the system
locale, checked in order
.BR LC_ALL " > " LC_MESSAGES " > " LANG :
German if the first value that is set starts with
.BR de ,
otherwise English \- including when no locale is set at all. The
.I lang
config key (or
.B SNOOZE_LANG
environment variable) can pin this to
.B de
or
.B en
regardless of the system locale; leave it empty for automatic detection.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Everything succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
At least one target was not found or failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error (e.g.
.B list
without a subcommand, an aborted confirmation prompt, an invalid duration
or scheduled time).
.TP
.B 130
Aborted via Ctrl\-C.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/snooze.conf
System\-wide configuration (mode 0640, owner root, read via ACL for
authorized admin users \- see
.BR SECURITY ).
.TP
.I /etc/snooze.conf.example
Annotated reference/template for the above; never read by
.B snooze
itself.
.TP
.I ~/.snooze.conf
Per\-user configuration, consulted if
.I ./snooze.conf
is absent.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR SNOOZE_URL ", " ZABBIX_URL
Override the
.I url
config key.
.TP
.BR SNOOZE_TOKEN ", " ZABBIX_TOKEN
Override the
.I token
config key. The recommended way to supply the token without a file on
disk at all (e.g. via a systemd
.IR EnvironmentFile ).
.TP
.B SNOOZE_DOMAIN
Override the
.I domain
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_DURATION
Override the
.I duration
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_PREFIX
Override the
.I prefix
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_TIMEOUT
Override the
.I timeout
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_RETRIES
Override the
.I retries
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_REASON
Override the
.I reason
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_CONFIRM_THRESHOLD
Override the
.I confirm_threshold
config key.
.TP
.B SNOOZE_LANG
Override the
.I lang
config key \- see
.BR LANGUAGE .
.SH SECURITY
The
.I token
is stored in plaintext wherever the config file lives. Access is controlled
through file permissions and, in the ACL\-based deployment this project
documents, a POSIX ACL granting read access to specific admin users rather
than making the binary or config world\-readable. When installed from this
package, no such restriction is applied automatically \- set it up to match
your own environment if the token must not be world\-readable, or supply
the token purely via
.B SNOOZE_TOKEN
/
.B ZABBIX_TOKEN
instead of a file.
